#!/bin/bash
# Refuse to update unless this machine verifies what it is about to install
# (SPEC.md §spec:os-updates).
#
# The build bakes a signing policy into the image: a sigstoreSigned entry for
# the published reference, the repository's public key at
# /etc/pki/containers/kantainer.pub, and the docker transport default set to
# reject. That policy is necessary and it is not sufficient, because bootc does
# not consult it by default. From `bootc switch --help`:
#
#   --enforce-container-sigpolicy
#       This is the inverse of the previous `--target-no-signature-verification`
#       (which is now a no-op).
#
# The mode is chosen when the machine is ATTACHED to the image and recorded in
# the deployment origin; `bootc upgrade` inherits it and never revisits it. So a
# machine attached without that flag pulls its nightly update with no signature
# check at all, and every part of the policy above sits there unread. Nothing on
# the machine fails, nothing is logged, and the operator sees a healthy box
# updating itself from a network location that could be anyone.
#
# This asks bootc what mode it is actually in and refuses to update if the
# answer is not "the container signing policy". bootc's own status is the
# verdict; the policy file is not re-read or re-derived here, because a second
# implementation of that judgement is the one that goes stale.
#
# It fails CLOSED, including on a status shape it does not recognise. A machine
# that stops updating says so - `systemctl status bootc-fetch-apply-updates`
# carries this text. A machine that updates unsigned says nothing, forever.

set -oue pipefail

if ! status="$(bootc status --json 2> /dev/null)"; then
    cat >&2 <<'MSG'
Update refused: `bootc status` did not report this machine's state.

Without it there is no way to tell whether this machine verifies the signature
on the image it is about to install, and installing without knowing is how an
unattended machine ends up running someone else's image.

Run `bootc status` on this machine to see what it says.
MSG
    exit 1
fi

signature="$(jq -r '.status.booted.image.image.signature // "none"' <<< "${status}")"

if [[ "${signature}" != "containerPolicy" ]]; then
    # The reference comes from the machine's own status, never from a literal
    # here. image.env is this project's single source for the published
    # reference (§spec:image-publication), and a copy of it written into a
    # shipped script is a copy that can disagree with the policy the image
    # carries - which is the failure that policy exists to prevent.
    booted="$(jq -r '.status.booted.image.image.image // "the published kantainer image"' <<< "${status}")"

    cat >&2 <<MSG
Update refused: this machine does not verify the signature on its updates.

  bootc reports its signature mode as: ${signature}
  This image's updates require:        containerPolicy

The signing policy and the repository's public key are baked into the image, but
bootc only consults them when the machine was attached to the image with
--enforce-container-sigpolicy. This machine was not, so its nightly update would
install whatever the registry served, signed or not.

Updates stay stopped until that is fixed. To fix it, re-attach this machine to
the image it is already running, with verification on:

  bootc switch --enforce-container-sigpolicy ${booted}

then reboot. Reinstalling from a current installer does the same thing.
MSG
    exit 1
fi
